This project includes or derives from the following third-party work.

1. T-matrix (EBCM) code
   tmatrix.py and tmatrix.js are ports of the Fortran routine ampld.lp.f by
   M. I. Mishchenko (NASA Goddard Institute for Space Studies), released into the
   public domain. The port was made from the copy distributed with the pytmatrix
   package (J. Leinonen). The authors of the original code request that its use be
   acknowledged by citing:
     - M. I. Mishchenko and L. D. Travis, "T-matrix computations of light
       scattering by large spheroidal particles", Opt. Commun. 109, 16-21 (1994).
     - M. I. Mishchenko, L. D. Travis and D. W. Mackowski, "T-matrix computations
       of light scattering by nonspherical particles: a review", J. Quant.
       Spectrosc. Radiat. Transfer 55, 535-575 (1996).
   The method is P. C. Waterman's extended boundary condition method (Proc. IEEE
   53, 805 (1965); Phys. Rev. D 3, 825 (1971)).

2. Lorenz-Mie conventions
   mie.py and mie.js follow the notation and conventions of C. F. Bohren and
   D. R. Huffman, "Absorption and Scattering of Light by Small Particles"
   (Wiley, 1983). The series truncation is W. J. Wiscombe's, "Improved Mie
   scattering algorithms", Appl. Opt. 19, 1505 (1980). No code from either
   source is included.

3. Red-blood-cell shape
   The biconcave profile is the Evans-Fung parametrisation: E. Evans and
   Y.-C. Fung, "Improved measurements of the erythrocyte geometry", Microvasc.
   Res. 4, 335-347 (1972).

4. Plotly.js (bundled in the built page only)
   The built HTML (dist/scattering_explorer.html, docs/index.html) inlines
   plotly.js, Copyright (c) Plotly, Inc., released under the MIT License
   (https://github.com/plotly/plotly.js/blob/master/LICENSE). Its copyright
   header is preserved inside the built file. Plotly is not included in the
   source files of this repository.

5. Optional test dependency
   validate.py optionally cross-checks against miepython (S. Prahl, MIT License),
   if installed. It is not bundled.

Refractive indices used in the presets and in rbc_shape_study.py are rounded,
illustrative values of the kind found in the standard literature and in the
RefractiveIndex.INFO database (https://refractiveindex.info). They are not
measured for any specific sample and carry no dispersion.
